1 NAME Michael /Henry/
1 SEX M
1 TITL Emigrant ancestor
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1683
2 PLAC Scotland
2 NOTE As written: about 1683
1 DEAT
2 DATE DEC 1760
2 PLAC Readington Twsp., Hunterdon Co., New Jersey
3 MAP
4 LATI N40.567
4 LONG W74.738
2 NOTE As written: Dec. 1760
1 BURI
2 PLAC Three Bridges, Hunterdon Co., New Jersey
3 MAP
4 LATI N40.523
4 LONG W74.797
1 NOTE Our ancestral emigrant to America was Michael Henry, of Readington Twsp., Hunterdon Co., New Jersey. He was b. about 1683, probably in Scotland. His parents at least were b. in Scotland, going from th
2 CONC ere to Newry, a seaport town in Counties Down and Armagh, Ireland. From Newry, Michael migrated to America, along with other Scotchmen who came in large numbers about the same time. He brought with hi
2 CONC m his wife, Jean or Jane, b. about 1694, also perhaps in Scotland, and certainly of Scotch parentage. Michael d. Dec. 1760 and was buried at Three Bridges Hunterdon Co., New Jersey. She d. June 1761 a
2 CONC nd was buried at Three Bridges.
2 CONT While no date of their coming to America has been found, it was probably in 1716, or earlier, as on Oct. 19 of that year he purchased of John Harrison a lot in the town of Perth Amboy, New Jersey, for
2 CONC  20 pounds. This lot was sold by Michael and Jean on 29 Dec. 1729. It is probable that Michael lived upon this lot until his removal to Hunterdon Co. While at Perth Amboy in 1718 he was appointed as a
2 CONC ssistant alderman of that city.
2 CONT Michael may have gone to Hunterdon Co. from Perth Amboy in 1732, or earlier. On 24 Dec. 1733 he purchased a farm of 137 acres in then Amwell, later Readington Twsp. Hunterdon Co., New Jersey, which be
2 CONC came the Henry homestead and is where Michael lived until his death. It was near Three Bridges, on the northerly side of the south branch of the Raritan River. (This farm descended to his son Nathanie
2 CONC l, by Michael’s will.) Subsequently he, Michael, purchased lands, probably adjoining. He also purchased on 13 Dec. 1743, 166 2/3 acres near Bloomsbury, which he sold Apr. 1752 to his son Michael. (Ano
2 CONC ther reference spells the name of this son, Micha.) These deeds show his thrift and business success.
2 CONT When he purchased the Emons farm in 1733, Michael was about 50 years of age, and, as he had been married since about 1714, or earlier (judging from his wife’s date of birth) various of his children mu
2 CONC st have been born at Perth Amboy, and perhaps the eldest, William, in Ireland. Only the younger of his eight children were probably born in Hunterdon Co., New Jersey. Michael signed his name to a deed
2 CONC  as ‘Michael Henarie.’ In 1733-34 Michael’s name was spelled ‘Hendry,’ but never so by himself.
2 CONT That Michael was a man of influence and high character is shown not only by the fact that he was a chosen freeholder of the new township at and after its formation in 1734 and 1735, and then an overse
2 CONC er of the poor in 1735, but by the further fact that, about the time of the organization of the First Presbyterian Church of Amwell (near Rezville, Hunterdon Co.) a meeting of the Presbytery of New Br
2 CONC unswick was held at his house 11 Oct. 1739. At this time Michael was an elder of the Amwell church, his name appearing second on the roll of elders of that church. He probably remained an elder for li
2 CONC fe (as the custom then was), as in 1756 his name again appears as one of the elders of the church who attended a meeting of the Presbytery. In 1749 Michael was one of the members of a lottery to raise
2 CONC  funds to finish the Presbyterian meeting house and to purchase a parsonage; he made a subscription to such personage.
2 CONT Michael died Dec. 1760 at the age of 77 years. He was buried, as was his wife later, in a burial plot near Three Bridges, in Hunterdon Co., New Jersey, on what we may believe to have been the farm on
2 CONC which he was living at the time of his decease. The plot lies between the New Jersey Central Railroad track and the South Branch River, and two elms still overshadow their graves. Other interments the
2 CONC re are of persons belonging to a Rowe family. A relationship between the Henry and Rowe families has not been established; it is possible a Rowe was a subsequent owner of the land.
2 CONT The inscription on Michael’s tombstone is as follows:
2 CONT Here lies the precious remains
2 CONT Michael Henry
2 CONT who died Dec. 1760
2 CONT aged 77 years
2 CONT The truly pious, faithful, loving friend
2 CONT Who persevered in every Christian virtue to the end
2 CONT To all his virtues neighbors and relatives dear
2 CONT Still loving them and serving God in fear
2 CONT But now he’s gone to praise his God above
2 CONT Where sorrows cease and nothing dwells but love.
2 CONT His consort, Jean, survived him only six months, and above her grave is a stone inscribed with the following:
2 CONT Here lies the precious remains
2 CONT Jean Henry, wife of
2 CONT Michael Henry who died
2 CONT June 1761, aged 67
2 CONT The accomplished friend, the Christian mother, wife,
2 CONT Shone with the brightest charms thro all her life.
2 CONT While death has broken every tender tie
2 CONT The friend and Christian will survive and
2 CONT Brighter shine on high.
2 CONT Michael’s will of 17 Jan. 1760 was probated 7 Jan. 1761 (Trenton Wills, Book 10, p. 540). This will, which states he is “of Reading Township,” names his wife Jean, and, so far as known, all his childr
2 CONC en, viz: William, Esther, Michael, Daniel, David, John, Nathaniel (now gone to sea), Margaret, and Sarah; his grandson John, son of his son Michael; his grandson Arthur, eldest son of his eldest son W
2 CONC illiam; and his son-in-law William Bishop. By this will he devises to his wife Jean “the northeast upper room in the house where I now dwell” (a not unusual bequest in those days) with choice of furni
2 CONC ture for it, and with provision that his son John shall pay her 15 lbs. yearly and sufficient meat, drink, and firewood, and with other provisions for her sustenance. To his son John he devises the tr
2 CONC act he purchased of Abraham Delemater and the widow Bogart; Nathaniel, the tract he purchased of Nicholas Emons; to his daughter Margaret and Sarah, the tract he purchased of John Rupert; and his libr
2 CONC ary books (which must have been numerous for that period), to his children, the Rev. James McCrea, of Lamington, to make the division of such books. The executors were his sons David and John, and Jac
2 CONC ob Mattison.
2 CONT An inventory of his personal estate, filed 5 Jan. 1761, amounted to 712.8.0 lbs. and included 22 horses and colts, 34 cattle, and 37 sheep, weave house and smoke house, spinning wheels, 2 guns, etc. O
2 CONC n 19 Nov. 1766 a final account was rendered by his son John Henry. The will of Jean (signed by mark) of 3 Mar. 1761, three months before her death, was not probated until 26 Nov. 1763 (Trenton Wills, 
2 CONC Book ll, p. 445). In it she mentions only her grandson Arthur Henry, son-in-law William Bishop, and son John, the two latest being her executors. Her small estate was settled 22 Jan. 1768.
2 CONT The true name of the wife of Michael Henry is not known. His will shows it as ‘Jean,’ and the foregoing reference of her will shows it as ‘Jean.’ In the references shown below her name is given as ‘Ja
2 CONC ne’:
2 CONT 1761, Mar. 5, Henry, Jane, of Reading Township, Hunterdon Co., Will of. Widow of Michael Henry. Grandson Arthur Henry 5 schillings. Son-in-law William Bishop. Witts. Nathaniel Henry, Sarah Henry. Prov
2 CONC ed Nov. 26 1763.
2 CONT Appraised property David Henry.
1 NOTE References: N.J. 1 Vol. 33, p. 188—Utah Genealogical Library, Salt Lake City, Utah. This same reference, on p. 187, gives a will of Michael Henry, Vol. 7, wherein Michael gives his wife’s name as ‘Jan
2 CONC e.’ Another reference shows her name was ‘Jane Robertson,’ but that too has not been proven to be correct.
1 NOTE References: A family tradition tells of Michael Henry bringing with him to America a fine large apple, or apple tree, which became known as the Michael Henry apple. Some descendants of this tree were
2 CONC long known in various parts of this state, Utah, and are said yet to be found in some sections.
1 NOTE References: There being no Bible or other records discovered giving the births or particulars of Michael Henry’s children (except of David), it has been difficult to give dates of birth or the full na
2 CONC mes of the wives of many of his nine children. The birth dates given below are therefore, for the most part, conjecture, and the order of the nine children is not quite certain:
