1 NOTE In the summer of 1896, James Henrie decided to leave Manti and look for a new home in Idaho. The family packed all their things in a covered wagon and the boys drove the livestock. They were a long ti
2 CONC me on the road, as James stopped occasionally to find work, and after the job was through he would continue on with his family.
2 CONT They thought of making their home several places along the way, but there seemed to be always something wrong with the locality. They finally came to the Teton Basin, then known as Pierre’s Hole. This
2 CONC  was the most famous place for the Rendezvous in Idaho. Herds of wild elk and deer grazed in the meadows. The grass grew as high as horses. The people had to keep close watch of their animals or cattl
2 CONC e so that they would not be killed by the bear and other fierce animals.
2 CONT Here they settled. The place later was named Clawson, Idaho. They took up a large tract of land and built a log house upon it. Farming and trading horses became James’ main occupation.
2 CONT They helped to organize a branch of the Church here; it was later known as Clawson Ward. James loved to sing and he lead all the singing. He also loved to take part in plays and did this very well. At
2 CONC  one time he and his daughter Mary Etta were in the play “Little Eva,” which was so well liked that they took the play to many communities.
2 CONT His wife, Hannah, died shortly after the birth of their son Hannah Roosevelt, causes incident to birth. James and a friend, Henry Lamoreaux, chose the site for the first cemetery, on a little hill ove
2 CONC rlooking the valley. (It is known as the Cache Clawson Cemetery. Here his wife was buried, on December 5, 1904, the first person interred.
2 CONT Margaret Jones.
