1 NOTE After their marriage, Della and Jarred made their home in Orangeville. Like the early pioneers, they took up a new plot of ground, which had to have the brush and rocks removed. It had to be leveled a
2 CONC nd ditches had to be made to get water to it. They worked together and built a 4-room house themselves out of hand-made adobes. Here their 7 children were born. Della served 9 yrs. in the Primary as t
2 CONC eacher and counselor. For the past 7 yrs. she has been secretary in Relief Society.
2 CONT Children of Della May and Jarred LeRoy Taylor, all b. in Orangeville, 7:
2 CONT Dean was a Marine in World War II. He fought on Guadalcanal and many
2 CONT other places in the Pacific Islands; was sent to New Zealand for 6 months in a rest camp. He gained all the promotions until he was Tech.Sgt.; was overseas 27 months. He had been very ill with malaria
2 CONC . After returning to the United States he married Bernice MacKay. They lived in California until March 1950, when they moved to Spanish Fork, Utah. They bought an unfinished home and completed it. Aft
2 CONC er living there a short time, he was made counselor in the bishopric.
2 CONT home in Price, Utah. They had 3 children, all b. in Price:
