1 NOTE with him. After all arrangements were made for the voyage, the friend decided not to come and Emil made the trip alone. He was so enthusiastic over what he saw that he decided to stay a year in Americ
2 CONC a. He remained and in time because a true American citizen. His first job was in a coal mine; starting at the bottom he at a very young age became mine inspector and superintendent for Utah Fuel Co. a
2 CONC nd held this position for about 30 years. While living in Carbon Co. Emil was elected County Commissioner for two terms. He was a stockholder in the Price 1st National Bank, Ford Motor Co., and Easter
2 CONC n Utah Elec. Co. After his marriage to Blanche he left the mining business and moved to Provo, where he affiliated with the dairy business on a big scale. He was not very happy in this venture and sol
2 CONC d out, and built a motion picture theater in Springville, Utah. He was privileged to return to Sweden to spend 3 months with his family and friends. When he returned to Springville he was elected City
2 CONC  Councilman for 3 years, also city Mayor for a term. He acquired the ownership of 2 theaters in Spanish Fork and 2 in Springville; has served as president of the Inter Mountain Theater Assn. He has se
2 CONC rved in various capacities in the Church. He reared 4 children by his first wife; one daughter filled a two-year mission in the Northwestern states. Blanche Henrie Ostlund enjoys keeping a lovely home
2 CONC  and is especially fond of art and music. She has served as a Bee Hive teacher, Gleaner Girl teacher, and President for 4 years of the Y.L.M.I.A; has been a counselor on the M.I.A. Stake Board; has wo
2 CONC rked in Relief Society, Sunday School; has been president of Alpha Beta Club (Ladies Literary), and First Lady of Springville during the time her husband was Mayor of the city.
2 CONT Blanche and Emil adopted a baby girl when she was only a few hours old:
2 CONT 18 Mar. 1928, in Springville, Utah.
2 CONT Jerrol was active in high school dramatics, an honor student of the Thespian Club and received many awards in speech contests. He was an honor high school graduate. He went on a 2½ year mission to Eng
2 CONC land and was made president of the Nottingham Dist. He served under his father who was president of the British Mission, for 4 years. After returning he attended the Brigham Young University for 2½ ye
2 CONC ars. He has been an active church worker, as teacher in the different auxiliaries, and counselor on the Mutual Stake Bd. He is a bookkeeper for Whiting & Heymond Constr. Co. and is still working on hi
2 CONC s degree in accounting.
2 CONT Norma Rae is talented in dramatics, dancing, and entertaining. She as a
2 CONT member of the Thespian Club and received the highest award of a “two-star
