2 NOTE As written: 22 Sept. 1924
1 NOTE offered only to better qualified men.
2 CONT From Ft. Benning he was shipped to England on the “Queen Elizabeth.” While at Ft. Benning he made 9 jumps, 1 in England, and 1 in France. In England he was assigned to the 101st Division and was in tr
2 CONC aining there from June to Sept. 1944. From England they flew to Holland to help drive the Germans out (here they were known as the Eagle Division). As he and two companions were landing by parachutes,
2 CONC  they were fired upon by a Ggerman artillary gun. They tried to hide in a ditch of dirty muddy water as the German was hot after them. They kept firing and shrapnel was flying all around,. Before the 
2 CONC boys ran the German out, a piece of shrapnel buried itself in Kent’s foot. A medic put him on a bicycle with a flat tire. He laughs when he tells about that ride, as it was hard to peddle and gain any
2 CONC  speed. A jeep took him to an airplane and he was flown to Brussels and put in a Catholic hospital for one day. From there he was flown back to England.
2 CONT A paragraph taken from a history of the 101st Airborne Division the day they made the jump in Holland: “Flack came a few minutes before the drop zones and many sticks of chutists leaped from flaming p
2 CONC lanes held steadily on course until all occupants were out. Later troopers lauded the courage of the pilots who held their planes in formation and even lessened speed during the jump in spite of inten
2 CONC se anti-aircraft fire. Only after the Airborne had left their ships did the pilots attempt to take any evasive action against the heavy ack-ack. In many cases it was too late and the drop zones were d
2 CONC otted with black smoke where their planes had crashed.” Kent was in the hospital for 3 months when one day the nurse removed the bandages from his foot and started the wound to hemorrhaging. They had 
2 CONC to hurry and tie the blood vessels without anesthetic for he was fast bleeding to death.
2 CONT After leaving the hospital in England he was sent back into combat to France and all through Germany. He was promoted to corporal, then sergeant, and staff sergeant. His division, the 101st with units
2 CONC  of French armies, captured the beautiful Berchtesgarden (Hitler’s Eagle Nest). Hitler and his officers were living there at the time; they had taken it from Austria. It is located in the Austrian Alp
2 CONC s. The boys had a rest period here and it was a paradise to battle- weary soldiers. Germans waited on them, with Nazi-wise discretion, and treated the conquerors like kings. Now travelers from all ove
2 CONC r the world visit this place as one of the most beautiful sight seeing spots in the world.
2 CONT After serving for 2½ years, Kent was discharged 3 Jan. 1946.
2 CONT After Kent returned home he worked at different jobs. He then decided to learn a trade so he went to work for Custom Floor Co., laying carpet, tile, linoleum, etc. He now has a paying business with hi
2 CONC s brother-in-law, Eldon F. Lewis.
2 CONT Wanda, his wife, went through the schools at Provo. She was a member of the Pep Club at high school and graduated from a business course. After her marriage she worked in the office at Ironton Plant i
2 CONC n Provo, and later in the office of the Security Title & Abstract Co. in Provo. Kent Devon & Wanda (Wright) LeaMaster had 2 children, b. in Provo, Utau:
