1 NAME Alice /Johnson/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 MAY 1889
2 PLAC Manti
3 MAP
4 LATI N39.268
4 LONG W111.636
2 NOTE As written: 7 May 1889
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 APR 1951
2 PLAC Manti
3 MAP
4 LATI N39.268
4 LONG W111.636
2 NOTE As written: 4 Apr. 1951
1 NOTE John R. Nielson Jr. was born of Norwegian parents who immigrated to Utah to be with people of the L.D.S. faith. They had a difficult time in a new country, learning a new language and new customs. The
2 CONC  children learned early how to help support themselves. At the age of twelve, John R. drove a four-horse team and loaded wagon to Nevada, where he lived with a family while proving up on a homestead e
2 CONC ntry. His education was interrupted before finishing high school, as he was compelled to go out to work. Much of his teen-age was spent in Nevada as a sheep herder for his brother-in-law. He was calle
2 CONC d from there to fill a mission in Norway, the land of his ancestors. When his son filled a mission in Norway forty years later, friends and converts spoke of his fluent speech and the wonderful sermon
2 CONC s he preached.
2 CONT After his marriage to Alice Johnson he became a grocery store partner to C. G. Braithwaite for about three years. He was not satisfied with the grocery business and decided he wanted to teach school.
2 CONC This necessitated returning to high school, and before the course was completed he was asked to take an emergency position as teacher, and remained with the profession for nineteen years. College cred
2 CONC its had then become a first requirement for teachers and he had to quit. He had a natural talent for teaching along with a good mind. In civic life he was city councilman, city recorder and mayor. He 
2 CONC supervised the extension and increase of the culinary water system, using W.P.A. labor. He took a farm census of southern Utah. He worked with youth on the N.Y.A.; was clerk of the O.P.A., and was one
2 CONC  of the organizers of the Utah Poultry Producers.
2 CONT In church affairs he was active and capable. He was superintendent of Manti North Ward Sunday School, stake superintendent of M.I.A., counselor to the bishop of North Ward, and teacher of parents’ cla
2 CONC ss in Sunday School.
2 CONT He was one of the first men of his community to build a mountain cabin for summer use. He was honored, loved, and respected as a teacher, always a friend to the down-trodden and poor. Above all he was
2 CONC  a teacher of righteousness and never tired of explaining the gospel. Few men were blessed with so keen a mind, sympathetic understanding, and faith. John R. and Alice had 7 children, all b. in Manti,
2 CONC  Utah:
2 CONT 1946.
2 CONT Scout and lover of the out-of-doors; came up through the Priesthood to calling of Seventy. He is what the family terms a “perfectionist” and “plodder.”
2 CONT Snow Jr. College and left to serve in the Army for 1½ years. He attended Utah State Agricultural College one year when he was called to go on a mission to Norway. There he found people who remembered
2 CONC his father who had been a missionary there forty years before. His father died before he was released from the field. When his mission was completed, he toured part of Europe: Sweden, Denmark, Germany
2 CONC , Switzerland, and France, and sailed home from England. He then entered Brigham Young University, but a change in his major has delayed his graduation and degree. He is a member of the choir, a Scout
2 CONC  Master, a Wad Teacher, and a Seventy.
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 7 May 1897, end. & S.to H. 25 June 1913; bapt. 3 Mar. 1896, end. 14 Sept. 1910
