1 NOTE Sarah was the first baby girl b. in the Fort at Panguitch after the second settlement of that place in 1871, which was then in Iron Co. Her husband’s father was one of the first teachers she had in sc
2 CONC hool. She married George in Panguitch and her father performed the ceremony. George was orphaned at 2 years of age and his father remarried and moved from Harmony to Panguitch. His father owned and op
2 CONC erated the first sawmill in Panguitch, located half way between Panguitch and Panguitch Lake, and was the only Democratic sheriff ever to be elected to the position in Panguitch.
2 CONT In 1938 Sarah and George were called as missionaries to the St. George Temple for 3 years. She was endowed for approximately 1100 women, worked in the baptismal room, helped with the temple clothing,
2 CONC and was sealed for thousands of couples and families, doing 50 couples at one time. George worked along the same lines as Sarah. They were released in 1941. For many years prior to the temple mission 
2 CONC they lived on a ranch, raising cattle, sheep, horses, and grain, and making butter and cheese.
2 CONT They reared with their family an orphan girl, Lucile Stone, who came to them. She shared with the others till her marriage.
2 CONT They celebrated their 62nd wedding anniversary 12 Jan. 1952 and George died 2 days later. Sarah is still living; she crochets, reads, and enjoys her family, many grandchildren, and hosts of friends. T
2 CONC hey had 10 children, all b. in Panguitch:
2 CONT 28 Nov. 1945, Long Beach, Calif., to Alvera Braze, dau. of George &
2 CONT Barbara Braze. She was b. 29 Oct. 1920, Chicago, Ill. They had 2
2 CONT children, b. in Long Beach
2 CONT a. Phillip George Dodds, b. 30 Aug. 1946. b. James William
2 CONT Dodds, b. 15 Mar. 1949.
2 CONT Thomas Ira & Christena (Cooper) Schow. She was b. 24 May 1926,
2 CONT a. Barbara Dodds, b. 26 Feb. 1951, Panguitch.
