1 NOTE father straightened her out and said, “She’s gone.” Her mother refused to give her up and said to her, “Berthie, Berthie, I can’t let you go.” She revived and lived to be 66 years old. As she grew int
2 CONC o womanhood and desired to help herself financially, she accepted a position as clerk in the Cameron-Sevy Mercantile Store. Thomas Sevy, the manager, said of her, “Clerks as good as Bertha are as scar
2 CONC ce as hen’s teeth.” Samuel A. Worthen, also a merchant of Panguitch and one time Bishop of his ward, said of her, “You can go through Bertha’s church records and never find a mistake.” She was honest 
2 CONC in all her dealings with everyone, and she was popular with her friends.
2 CONT One 24th of July she went with a crowd to Panguitch Lake to celebrate Pioneer day. Crowds gathered from far and near at this resort for a two weeks outing of fishing, horse racing, and dancing. Horace
2 CONC  Miller was one of the group. The wagon was loaded with food and camp supplies. When the party was in the wagon and on the way, Horace gave Bertha a large box, containing an accordion, saying, “It is 
2 CONC yours if you can learn to play it before we reach the lake.” She did learn and was playing tunes for the crowd.
2 CONT The remainder of her life, Bertha was noted for her accordion and her ability to play it. She freely entertained at programs, church functions, parties, and on all occasions when invited to do so. Som
2 CONC e of her favorite numbers were, “Redwing,” “Sweet Sings the Donkey,” “Listen to the Mocking Bird,” a schottische with a lot of bass, all of the patriotic and modern numbers, as well as church music. S
2 CONC he was also talented at the organ and taught young people to play, during the early part of her married life.
2 CONT One day before her marriage she was riding down main street on a tall horse in a side saddle. The horse became excited and she was thrown to the ground and suffered a brain concussion.
2 CONT She was chosen to represent the Goddess of Liberty for the 4th of July celebration in 1897. Bertha enjoyed hunting and fishing and was very good at shooting. She loved the mountains and canyons, all o
2 CONC f nature, and the out-of-doors.
2 CONT She and Horace Miller were married in the Manti Temple in 1897. The trip by team and covered wagon from Panguitch to Manti in December was a long and cold journey During the return trip they had the m
2 CONC isfortune of setting their bedding on fire with the hot bricks they had tucked down in the wagon to help keep them warm They were loaded with freight on the return trip and suffered with the cold and 
2 CONC slowness of travel.
2 CONT She affiliated herself with the different organizations of the church as long as her health would permit. She was counselor in the M.I.A., organist and secretary of Primary, organist in the Sunday Sch
2 CONC ool, secretary-treasurer of Relief Society, and a charter member of the Daughters of the Utah Pioneers. Quilting was an art in her day and she did a great deal of it for the Relief Society and for fam
2 CONC ily and friends.
2 CONT She was kindness itself to her family and the friends of her children; hey were always welcome to take friends home whenever they desired. She as also called into many homes to care for the sick. When
2 CONC  her son Francis’ oldest daughter was born, the mother almost lost her life and it was Bertha ho sat by her bed and nursed her back to health.
2 CONT Her grandchildren loved to visit her and enjoyed hearing her play. Although some of the grandchildren lived nearby and some farther away, she never once showed partiality for anyone of them above the
2 CONC others. Her oldest I on Horace Jr. once said to her, when home on a visit, “Mother, these children of mine are the cutest grandchildren you have aren’t they?” She relied, “They are some of the nicest 
2 CONC grandchildren I have,” and she meant it. hey were all very special to her.
2 CONT Some of the choicest memories that her family treasures were her gentleness and understanding, her great love for her family and her desire that each should grow up to be an honorable Latter-day Saint
2 CONC  and good citizen. he was composed and helpful when the family needed it most. She lived the things she taught and her family reveres her memory and mourns her passing.
2 CONT Horace Miller was the third child of the family. Most of his life was spent in Panguitch, but on occasions he spent a short time at his Grandmother Smith McGregor’s home in Parowan. During his young m
2 CONC anhood he spent the summers with his parents and brothers and sisters at the Blue Spring Ranch, southwest of Panguitch Lake. His schooling was limited to a few years, but he gained much practical know
2 CONC ledge during his life. He knew almost very mountain, valley, spring and stream throughout southern Utah. He helped his father with the livestock and farm chores.
2 CONT At the Blue Spring Ranch one summer he climbed a quaking asp tree that is brother and sister were cutting down. He lost his footing and slid down he trunk to the cut in the tree, just as the ax cut in
2 CONC to the notch, and his foot was almost severed. Wagon grease was used to stop the bleeding and the wound was a long time healing.
2 CONT When about 13 years of age his father gave him a shoe repairing kit and a barber comb and clippers and told him it was his job henceforth to keep the family barbered and well shod. This he did faithfu
2 CONC lly and throughout his life carried on his trade when occasion required. With wagon and team he freighted goods and commodities from Salina when the D.& R.G. Railroad extended only that far south, and
2 CONC  later from Marysvale, 50 miles from Panguitch. In his early married life he cared for sheep for Ed Heywood. He was with them at Mt. Trumbell in Arizona in the winter months, and the summer range was 
2 CONC at East Fork adjacent to Panguitch. He managed to have his family with him part of each summer. Later he accumulated some livestock for himself and some farm land which lay about 7 miles north of Pang
2 CONC uitch. He was then able to spend more time with his family.
2 CONT He worked very hard to provide for his growing family, yet found time to help dig ditches, build churches, and do other things where his team and wagon could be put to use. He owned good teams and sad
2 CONC dle ponies, which his children enjoyed riding and were very proud of. The children love to recall the days they spent at the summer herd. Horace bent trees down for teeters for them to play on, and th
2 CONC e children scampered about the camp. One day as they were moving the camp from one location to another, Bertha the mother sat in the wagon until it was loaded for the move. Just before starting Horace
2 CONC  helped her from the wagon to rest a bit. She had no sooner left the wagon when a bolt of lightning struck close by. It frightened the team and it ran away, scattering camp supplies all along the way 
2 CONC down the hill, rice, beans, bedding, canned goods, clothes, etc. The team finally were stopped in the trees. On one of the yearly community treks to Panguitch Lake for a 4th of July celebration, Horac
2 CONC e took his team and covered wagon and proceeded to the Lake. His daughter Bergetta and a girl friend went horseback. Thelma was employed as a clerk in the Garfield Exchange Store and could not leave u
2 CONC ntil her day’s work was done. When the girls arrived at camp, Horace had everything in readiness for them. He did most of the cooking while they were there and made everything very comfortable for the
2 CONC m.
2 CONT He loved to fish when Panguitch Lake was frozen over. It was the method of fishermen to cut holes in the ice, and they usually came away with more than the allowed limit. The children love to recall t
2 CONC he large trout frying in a huge pan and scarcely being able to restrain their hunger. Horace probably drove an automobile only once. He and his brother Jim were hauling some hay and Jim had to do a fe
2 CONC w chores before starting home. Horace became impatient at the delay, slid under the wheel, and started the car. He stepped on the gas and “was off.” He kept his foot on the gas and bounced through dit
2 CONC ches and over hay piles until he finally circled around toward his grandson Bevan. He shouted at the boy, “How do you turn it off?” Bevan was almost hysterical with laughter, but managed to shout back
2 CONC , “Turn off the key.” Jim also had his share of laughter over the incident.
2 CONT One of Horace’s outstanding qualities was his gentleness and efficiency in a sick room. It seemed to be a family characteristic, as his brothers were good nurses and always willing to help where men,
2 CONC especially, were ill and needed assistance. His mother was ill a good part of her life and. no doubt, the boys acquired their training in helping to care for her. Later his wife had a heart attack and
2 CONC  he gave her the most thoughtful and tender care throughout the remainder of her life.
2 CONT Another notable characteristic of Horace was his gentleness and love for his children and grandchildren. After the death of his wife he spent a good deal of time visiting his children but always retur
2 CONC ned to his home and was I happy to be there.
2 CONT He loved to visit his daughter Thelma and her husband at Cedar City, and to their farm. He was very much interested in the machinery Thurman made. Horace had sold his farm near the Panguitch cemetery
2 CONC and the Miller pasture his sons Francis and Mac. Thus he had no particular ties to hold him at home. He enjoyed his children and grandchildren and took an interest in all they did. He was very fond of
2 CONC  horse racing, basket ball games, and stock shows. When they were held in the vicinity he was sure to be present.
2 CONT When not visiting his children, he lived alone, until his death. All his children were at his side when he died in 1951. He lies buried by his wife in the Panguitch city cemetery. Horace & Bergetta (H
2 CONC enrie) Miller had 7 children, all b. in Panguitch:
2 CONT * 7 Mc Henrie Miller, b. 31 Aug. 1916; m. Orene-O’Rene Sudweeks.
