2 NOTE As written: 1 Oct. 1901
1 NOTE Her childhood days were spent in Panguitch on a farm, where she learned to do all the tasks a farm girl is required to do, milking, tending chickens, riding horses, etc. After the death of her first h
2 CONC usband, she returned to school for a time. Later she found work as a clerk in the Cameron & Sevy Mercantile Store at Panguitch, for 5 yrs.
2 CONT She has held responsible positions in the Church. She first began serving in the Primary and at different times was play leader, teacher, and stake counselor. In the M.I.A. she was stake secretary-tre
2 CONC asurer; in Sunday School she was secretary and teacher; in Relief Society she has been a visiting teacher, teacher, assistant treasurer, and assistant chorister. She has been a stake missionary for 2 
2 CONC yrs., also a missionary to the St. George Temple where she performed work for 61 women.
2 CONT She sang in Sunday School and Sacrament Meeting choirs from the time she was a very small girl. All her life she has loved to sing, especially in duets, trios, and quartets. She was a member of the Si
2 CONC nging Mothers group of the Stake and sang in the Salt Lake Tabernacle. She is a member of a Bazooka Band, which has entertained for the D.U.P. Convention at the Hotel Utah and over radio station KSL. 
2 CONC At Cedar City they broadcasted over KSUB. They have been invited to entertain at many places, weddings, family reunions dances, etc. They play frequently to raise funds for the Primary. She has been c
2 CONC horister of the D.U.P. camp and president of the same for 4 yrs., and presently is acting as chorister for the organization.
2 CONT Since the death of her second husband she has helped in rearing some of her grandchildren.
2 CONT Joseph Allen & Maria Eva Dean (Henrie) Bell had 3 children, b. in Panguitch:
2 CONT Iris and David had 3 children, b. in Panguitch:
