2 NOTE As written: 12 July 1884
1 NOTE Marion was member 25 in a polygamist family of 30, the third son of the third wife. He had a “cold” reception as it is reported that all the crops in Panguitch Valley froze to the ground the morning h
2 CONC e was borne. He lived in Panguitch until 1889, when his parents moved to Fredonia, Arizona, to hide from the U.S. deputy marshals who wanted to take his father into court for living in polygamy. He ha
2 CONC d been fined once and the marshals wanted to get him again, to give him a prison term.
2 CONT His school days were spent in Fredonia, where he lived until he was 21, with the exception of five summers when he was running his mother’s farm in Panguitch, batching and cooking for himself. He took
2 CONC  the farm over at the age of 15, and freighted and worked with the sheep in the winters when he was not in school. Ha would have graduated with the first graduates from the 8th grade in Fredonia, but 
2 CONC he had to leave for Panguitch to put in the spring crops 15 days before the graduation exercises.
2 CONT After their marriage, Marion and Artemisia lived in Fredonia, Ariz., and Panguitch, Cannonville, and Delta, Utah. Wherever they have lived they have made many dear friends and taken an active part in
2 CONC church and civic affairs.
2 CONT In the fall of 1907, Marion was called on a mission to the Central States, where ha labored under President Samuel O. Bennion. At the age of 22 he was ordained a High Priest and labored in the Panguit
2 CONC ch Stake High Council several years. He later filled two short-term stake missions. He has been president of the Y.M.M.I.A., Chairman of Church Building Committee for both ward and stake, Teacher of t
2 CONC he Gospel Doctrine Class for 5 years, also a Teacher of the High Priests Quorum. In civic affairs, he was president of Cannonville Irrigation Co., also president of the Livestock Assn. there. In Delta
2 CONC  he worked for the State Road Commission and brought the first heavy road equipment into Millard Co. In the fall of 1933 he was elected County Commissioner and he worked hard to get the homes and land
2 CONC s back into possession of the people who lost on account of over-drainage, delinquent taxes, and the depression of the 1930's.
2 CONT Levings & Mable Augusta (Lytle) Dexter. She was b. 2-12 Apr. 1916, San
2 CONT end. & S.to H. 4 May 1932]. They had 2 children, b. in Delta, Utah:
2 CONT 1950]; m. 4 Aug. 1950, to Mont Shepard.
