2 NOTE As written: 5 Nov. 1891
1 NOTE Edgar and Frances lived across the street from each other; she used to pal around with his sister Myra and he with her brother Lindsay. At the age of 19 Frances went to Beaver to school at Murdock Aca
2 CONC demy and her family move to Delta, Utah. One Sunday after Church she was very lonely and decided to write a few letters, one especially to her sister Abbie who was living in Panguitch with her Grandmo
2 CONC ther Steele. As Frances was looking through some cards she had, she ran on to one that pictured a boy and girl with a suitcase and it read, “I’ll leave my happy home for you.” To have a little fun she
2 CONC  addressed this to Edgar, but when she went to post it she changed her mind and slipped it into her sister’s letter. When her sister opened the letter and read the card, she was disgusted and was goin
2 CONC g to burn it. However, her Aunt Emily Steele asked for it and took it home. She saw Edgar going downtown and called to him to come to her place that night to a cottage meeting. She said she had someth
2 CONC ing for him, and when he did come, she gave him the card. It was not long until Frances received a letter from him and from then on they corresponded with each other and later married.
2 CONT They moved to Panguitch to live, but as the Hatchtown reservoir had broken and had taken most of their farm, they were not satisfied to stay there and moved into the Delta country, Delta, Utah. They b
2 CONC ought 40 acres of brush land, cleared it, and planted it into wheat. It grew and looked like there would be a fine crop. One day as Ed was walking through the grain, he picked a few heads off and rubb
2 CONC ed them in his hands, but there were no kernel of wheat, just chaff. All through the 40 acres it was that way, so they left it and bought another farm that was growing good crops, 5 miles closer into 
2 CONC Delta. They lived there for about 4 years when they had the opportunity to buy the best farm in the valley, which they did. However, there was something on this farm that gave Frances asthma, so they 
2 CONC had to leave. They rented the farm and moved into the town of Delta. In 1927 the banks closed and people who owed them could not pay. Because of this situation, they lost their farm and moved to Marys
2 CONC vale, Utah to the Elbow Ranch to work for John L. Sevy Sr. Francis Henrie (Ed’s brother) and his family moved there also. They took over the Sevy sheep. But there came a slump in the sheep business wh
2 CONC ich put them in debt so far they decided there was not enough left for both families, so they pulled out and let Francis have it. They then went in partnership with Ivan L. Foisy in the Utah Poultry b
2 CONC usiness. They were there about 2 years when Foisy closed down his business and they were forced to move back to Panguitch, where they rented the Showalter Ranch and lived there 5 years. While there Fr
2 CONC ances made butter and cheese. Then they bought Ed’s mother’s old home and moved into town, where they now live. Throughout his life, Edgar has been active in Church affairs, as counsel or and presiden
2 CONC t of Y.M.M.I.A. for 4 years; secretary and coordinator of the Aaronic Priesthood for 2 years; stake missionary, 3 times; counselor in the Sunday School of Panguitch South Ward, 4 years; scoutmaster fo
2 CONC r many years; chairman of the South Ward Genealogical Committee; ward teacher most of his life; and temple worker Frances has also been very active in Church works in Relief Society, as treasurer for 
2 CONC 8 years; magazine agent, 2 years; class leader, 1 year; chorister, 2 years; counselor, 6 years; and work director, 11 years. In Primary, as counselor for 4½ years; teacher, 12 years; and chorister, 8 
2 CONC years. In Sunday School, as teacher, 12 years. She has also served as a stake missionary for 2 years; stake genealogy counselor, 3 years; secretary and treasurer of ward genealogy, 1 year; secretary o
2 CONC f the choir, 3 years; choir member, 40 years; and chorister in the Y.L.M.I.A. for 3 years.
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 5 Nov. 1899, end. 17 June 1914; bapt. 8 May 1901, end. & S.to H. 17 June 1914
