2 DATE 5 SEP 1918
2 PLAC Parowan, Iron Co., Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.842
4 LONG W112.828
2 NOTE As written: 5 Sept. 1918
1 NOTE After graduating from Parowan High School, he took advantage of a small scholarship to Branch Agricultural College at Cedar City, for 2 winters. To help him complete that schooling, his mother rented
2 CONC her home in Parowan and the two moved into a one-room apartment in Cedar City. In addition to the one room, they had the use of a bath and large screen porch with a family of five. He slept on the por
2 CONC ch and well remembers the winter mornings when he would awaken to find sometimes several inches of snow on his bed, but he did not mind it. To make the ends meet more easily, he worked at the J.C. Pen
2 CONC ney Store each evening and Saturdays during the summers they were there.
2 CONT After graduating from the B.A.C. he entered the University of Utah. This was the move he had been contemplating since he was a youngster in grade school. He had a small scholarship the first year. The
2 CONC  first two winters at the U. of U. he worked in a boarding house of 18 boys. He was busy from 6 00 A.M. until class time serving breakfast and clearing up after it. Evenings were busy until 7:00; then
2 CONC  he would study until 12:00 and sometimes 2:00 A.M. The third winter he worked for a family, which was more pleasant and much easier than the boarding house. One phase of this work included taking the
2 CONC ir youngest son and the neighborhood’s youngsters skiing each suitable weekend.
2 CONT The summer vacation of 1939 he spent driving a bus for the Utah Parks Co through the scenic areas of Southern Utah. The following summer, 1940, was spent with a Forest Service road crew in the Uinta M
2 CONC ountains. He graduated from the University of Utah in 1941, with a B.A. degree in Chemistry. He left the next day for the East Coast. There he contacted the Hercules Powder Co. at Wilmington, Delaware
2 CONC , and was hired pretty much on the spot. After working for them one month in their experimental station at Wilmington, he was transferred to a smokeless powder plant at Belvidere, N.J. This plant was 
2 CONC being operated for the British Government and was producing powders for small cannons, machine guns, and rifles. He worked a few months in the analytical control laboratory, a year in the safety depar
2 CONC tment, and 6 months in the production development group. From then until Feb. 1944 he was production shift supervisor, actually in charge Or the production of powder.
2 CONT The Belvidere plant closed and he was transferred to the Radford Ordnance Works, Radford, Va. A month later he returned to Pennsylvania and married Minnie Anna Miller, whom he met over the telephone d
2 CONC uring the midnight to 8:00 A.M. shift. She was the plant operator and visited with the personnel. She had 3 sons by a former marriage. When school was out she and the boys moved to Va. for a time, the
2 CONC n they returned to Penn.
2 CONT Thomas left Hercules Oct. 1945 to work for the Carbide and Carbon Chemical operation at Oak Ridge, Tenn., in the Atomic Energy plant called K-25. He was transferred to the Manhattan Engineer District
2 CONC U.S. Corps of Engineers, working again for the government. This District was the group which became the Atomic Commission when it was formed in Jan. 1947. He was placed in the SF Accountability Branch
2 CONC . The responsibility in general was to maintain control of all the Source and Fissionable (SF) materials charged to the Oak Ridge Operations Office. The job was unique in that it had no comparable cou
2 CONC nterpart in normal industry. It involved a knowledge of the chemistry of these new elements, nuclear physics, and accounting.
2 CONT In Oct. 1949 he was transferred from the Oak Ridge Operations Office of the Atomic Energy Commission to Santa Fe Operations Office. The work remained essentially the same, but the security and respons
2 CONC ibility increased because he was now dealing with finished atomic weapons. Oct. 1951 the Santa Fe Operations moved headquarters to Albuquerque, New Mex. Here they purchased a home, which they aim to k
2 CONC eep even if other moves are in the future. Minnie was in charge of the Admission Office at the Los Alamos Medical Center for approximately a year while they lived there. She began to work for the Atom
2 CONC ic Energy Commission in July 1951 and has been with them since. She is in charge of the classified mail and records room.
2 CONT Thomas James Haycock Jr. has adopted the 3 sons of Minnie Anna Miller by her former marriage. He feels sure that they will join the L.D.S. Church when they are out of the service:
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 6 Nov. 1926; bapt. 3 Oct. 1953
