1 NAME Fern Olive /Henrie/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 MAY 1897
2 NOTE As written: 12 May 1897
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 JAN 1919
2 PLAC Panguitch
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.823
4 LONG W112.436
2 NOTE As written: 14 Jan. 1919
1 NOTE Fern was the only girl of a family of seven; six boys were younger than her. She had a happy childhood and was petted and adored by her brothers. In return, she was affectionately devoted to her famil
2 CONC y. Being industrious, she found great joy and satisfaction in helping with the household duties and at various times would don brownies and help on the farm, especially at haying time when she would r
2 CONC ide the derrick horse. Her sunny disposition and merry laughter made her a favorite also among the children at school. Often she would be found at Christmas, Valentines Day, etc., surrounded by a bunc
2 CONC h of smaller children, helping them to make holiday candies and gifts.
2 CONT She was especially interested in music, which came quite natural and easy for her. She could play the piano, accordion, guitar, and harmonica. She was sustained assistant organist in Sunday School.
2 CONT After graduating from District School, she married James Ray Slade in the Manti Temple. The following year a baby girl was born to them, named Rae, but she lived only a few hours. What a disappointmen
2 CONC t and sorrow this was to Fern as she had no sister and had looked forward to the event, but the Lord giveth and the Lord taketh, and we must bow the head and say, “Thy will be done.”
2 CONT During the influenza epidemic at the close of World War I, 1919, Fern was called by death. Her husband, Ray, had flu at the same time, but he recovered and filled a 2 year mission to the Western State
2 CONC s.
2 CONT On 3 Oct. 1922 Ray m. (2) Brenda Worthen, who departed this life 27 Dec. 1928. Four children were born to them, 3 sons and 1 daughter. Some time later he married Brenda’s sister, Magda Worthen Warren,
2 CONC  who had previously lost her husband by death. One child, a boy, was born to them. They resided in Ogden.
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 1 July 1905, end. & S.to H. 6 July 1916; bapt. 20 June 1903, end. 6 July 1916
