2 NOTE As written: 7 Feb 1952
1 NOTE After graduating from high school, Vira entered Brigham Young University as a business student. She also studied business at the University of Utah. She became a skillful typist and won all her typing
2 CONC  credits. She worked in the school offices part-time as a typist, to help finance her education. After her marriage she continued working till her first child was born. They made their home in San Die
2 CONC go, Calif., where they purchased a laundry establishment and Vira did the bookkeeping and banking.
2 CONT Vira has been diligent in all Church affairs. Her husband was made bishop and her duties multiplied. Both and ward and stake were building a chapel and the women of the ward sponsored church suppers,
2 CONC bazaars, and other activities to raise money. Vira was one of the foremost in helping. She was also a member of the choir and a chorus.
2 CONT Their laundry establishment installed a new boiler, and when it was being tested it exploded, killing Eldredge and two other men. This left Vira to carry on the business and care for her home and chil
2 CONC dren.
2 CONT Eldredge was a very outstanding young man, an intelligent leader in all his affairs. Throughout his life he was active in church work: was a member of the Sunday School superintendency, president of t
2 CONC he Y.M.M.I.A., and at the time of his death was Bishop of North Park Ward, San Diego. He had a deep respect for authority and was active in defending his convictions. His was a life of service Vira an
2 CONC d Eldredge had 3 children, b. in San Diego, Calif.:
