2 DATE 1 JUL 1920
2 PLAC Panguitch
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.823
4 LONG W112.436
2 NOTE As written: 1 July 1920
1 NOTE Melvin Ballard EXCELL, 9th child of Henry George & Ane Marie (Henrie) Excell, was b. 6 Nov. 1921, Panguitch, d. 11 July 1939. He spent his life on the farm. In his youth he did not seem to have the fa
2 CONC mily gift of singing and the others always told him he was out of tune. Put he persisted in joining the chorus, and his father said to him, “Keep right on singing and some time you will be able to sho
2 CONC w them how it is done.” He did keep trying and developed a fine bass voice, which he used for the entertainment of groups in church, in the home, and wherever he went.
2 CONT Melvin attended high school for 3 years and would have been valedictorian had he lived. He was on the basket ball team and was a brilliant student.
2 CONT He graduated from Seminary, after he and some of his pals had made up with the teacher for some prank. It seems that they had not done the work outlined by the teacher and tried to talk him out of it.
2 CONC  The teacher told them if they did not make up the work, they would get a blank diploma. As soon as the graduating ceremony was over they slipped out, not receiving congratulations from anyone. They b
2 CONC ought material, went to a hotel room, did the required work, and went hack to the teacher. He gave them their diplomas and they all went home. When Ballard came into the house he said, “Well, her is m
2 CONC y diploma, I wouldn’t have let you see that blank one for any price.” Then he told his family how sorry he was for his behavior. He received a good scolding and was laughed at for doing anything so fo
2 CONC olish. Then he was praised for his attitude and receiving the award.
2 CONT His death resulted from a broken neck while diving into a swimming hole. The sand had shifted from one side of the pool to the other from the time he was swimming a few days before. He was paralyzed f
2 CONC rom the neck to his feet. The doctor reported that nothing could have been done to save him. Don, his brother, applied artificial respiration for six hours, with a little help from two other men, but 
2 CONC it was to no avail. The doctor worked over Don to keep him alive, because he became so exhausted and was heart broken. They were loving pals as well as brothers.
2 CONT Melvin was buried in the Panguitch cemetery. He was a dutiful and obedient lad, and was loved dearly. He loved his classmates and all with whom he associated. He had a good word for everyone and would
2 CONC  not listen to degrading remarks about others.
