2 NOTE As written: 3 Aug. 1913
1 NOTE After high school graduation, Arda returned to school for another year to study advanced needle craft and chorus. Since then she has learned to do the bookkeeping in her husband’s wholesale and retail
2 CONC  grocery business.
2 CONT She has performed the usual church duties that come to every member of the church if he but accept the calling. One memorable thing was taking a group of boys and girls from Flagstaff to the Mesa Temp
2 CONC le to perform the ordinance of baptism for the dead.
2 CONT The 19 Nov. 1944 was also a memorable day for both Arda and her husband. They had gone to Snowflake, Ariz., a distance of 135 miles, to attend Stake Conference. Ralph left early to return home with th
2 CONC e Bishop, Elder Benson, Nickolas G. Smith, Brother Lund, and others; and Arda drove the car home. The auto was loaded, as usual, for they never go to any Stake meeting in a partially loaded car. They 
2 CONC had two flat tires on the trip back and began to wonder if they would ever reach home. Ralph and others were to be ordained, Ralph as a High Priest, and Arda was just sure they would already have rece
2 CONC ived the ordination, and she did so much want to be there to witness his promotion. She finally arrived at 11:15 P.M. and they were waiting for her. She was truly thankful, for this was the first Apos
2 CONC tle ever to visit their home and she wanted to be there to hear every word. After the ordinations were performed, they sat down to a midnight supper which had been prepared by her mother and other sis
2 CONC ters of the ward. Elder Benson and Brother Smith stayed with them that night.
2 CONT In April 1945 they were again visited by another of the Apostles, Mathew Cowley. Her mother was very ill and Elder Cowley blessed her and told her she should live to be a comfort to her children, and
2 CONC she is still with them.
2 CONT Arda and Ralph moved to Kingman, Ariz., in 1946 and found plenty of work to be done in the branch there. There were few Saints and everyone had something to do. For 13 Sundays after regular exercises
2 CONC they studied the Articles of Faith, by Talmage, which was interesting and profitable. One member of the branch was an outsider who would come for a few Sundays and then attend the Baptist Church for a
2 CONC  time, to compare the doctrines.
2 CONT They held various religious services in their home, from the Primary to the Relief Society, and had the privilege of entertaining many of the presiding authorities of the Church, stake presidents, bis
2 CONC hops, and others. They also enjoyed entertaining the children and college students, a clean, fine group of young people, all interested in the work of the Church. August 1947 they were privileged to a
2 CONC dopt a lovely baby boy. Arda was Relief Society president at the time but was able to care for the baby and attend to the duties of her calling. The women served dinners, had food sales, banquets, and
2 CONC  did a great deal of sewing, to raise money for the completion of the church building.
2 CONT April 1950 they were again privileged to adopt another baby. They had hoped it would be a girl. They made preparations to receive the child and felt they would be happy if it were either a boy or a gi
2 CONC rl. The baby was not born till the 5th of June and they were very happy to add a little girl to their family. They are very thankful for these two lovely children. Their little branch is growing in sp
2 CONC irit and increasing in numbers. Some non-Mormon children are in attendance at most of the junior meetings, which helps the work along. They feel humble and thankful for their blessings and membership 
2 CONC and are striving to obey and keep all the commandments required. Their 2 adopted children:
