1 NAME Maxine Excell /Cameron/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 APR 1918
2 PLAC Panguitch, Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.823
4 LONG W112.436
2 NOTE As written: 21 Apr. 1918
1 NOTE During the next year she worked as a telephone operator for Mountain States Telephone Co. On 30 Apr. 1937 she married Fearl in the Salt Lake Temple. He was working for her mother on the truck line, so
2 CONC  they continued to live at Panguitch. After her mother sold the line, Maxine and Fearl moved to Salt Lake and Fearl continued to drive trucks. His employer sent them back East to bring back some truck
2 CONC s for him. They visited several car plants in Detroit and Flint, Michigan. They were also privileged to attend the World’s Fair being held in New York City. It was an educational and impressive trip f
2 CONC or both of them.
2 CONT They again moved to Panguitch and Fearl decided to change employment and try driving a bus for a while. In 1942 he began driving for Sante Fe Bus Lines. This line later became the Continental Western
2 CONC Lines.
2 CONT They moved to Flagstaff, Ariz., in 1943, and soon thereafter Fearl was inducted into the service in World War II. He was gone 2 years, spending one year in Germany. Her sister Nila’s husband was also
2 CONC in service so they pooled their resources and lived together. Nila kept the home and cared for the children while Maxine worked for her brother-in-law in a grocery store. The experiences she gained an
2 CONC d the friends she made while working there have been some of the highlights of her life, particularly her association with the Indian people. She learned a little of their language before her husband 
2 CONC returned and she discontinued her work. He was employed by the same company and they continued to live in Flagstaff, where they have purchased a home and now reside.
2 CONT At this time Maxine began to realize how much her membership in the Latter-day Saint Church meant to her, the privileges extended to her to work in and for the Church, and she began to enjoy Church se
2 CONC rvice very much. She had worked in some of the organizations of the Church previous to this time, but it was not until now that she began to realize what true happiness can come to one, in the service
2 CONC  of the Lord.
2 CONT She has been a teacher in Primary, Relief Society, and Sunday School; activity counselor in M.I.A. and then president of the organization for 5 years; Relief Society president; literary leader and vis
2 CONC iting teacher of Relief Society. She and Fearl are presently Improvement Era directors for the M.I.A.
