1 NOTE and all other possessions they had and left the country, leaving her absolutely destitute. Her (3) marriage was not successful and again she divorced. After a few years of harder struggle to care for
2 CONC her 4 children, she married (4) Jesse Jean Skinner, as his second wife. Jess was a good man and they lived together fairly well for 15 years, when he had a stroke and became bedfast for the last seven
2 CONC  years of his life. Louisa cared for him as long as her health would permit, and then he was taken to the hospital, where he remained till his death. He had worked many years at the mines in Ely and w
2 CONC as eligible for hospital care. His body was taken to Beaver, Utah, for burial.
2 CONT Since Jess died, and again Louisa is alone, she has had the opportunity of traveling to some adjoining states and passing through some beautiful country. She has made many friends and spends considera
2 CONC ble time working in the St. George Temple.
2 CONT Rossmus and Louisa (Henrie) Lynn had 3 children, all b. in Panguitch:
2 CONT He was b. 22 Jan. 1902, Minersville, Beaver Co., Utah. They had 2 children, b. in Milford, Utah:
2 CONT a. Kenneth Ralph Hegland, b. 4 Oct. 1947, Delano, Kern Co.,
2 CONT Calif.
2 CONT b. Sandra Lee Hegland, b. 16 Mar. 1950, Delano.
2 CONT 5 Oct. 1941]; m 6 Feb. 1953, to Grant Truman. Joyce and Grant are
2 CONT attending college.
