0 @I3775@ INDI
1 NAME Veryl Clayton /Henrie/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 31 DEC 1910
2 PLAC Garland, Box Elder Co., Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N41.741
4 LONG W112.161
2 NOTE As written: 31 Dec. 1910
1 NOTE Veryl found great pleasure in extracurricular school activities, especially in sports. During his freshman year in high school, he was happy in the experience of wielding the brush that painted the se
2 CONC nior class president green. Four years later this compliment was returned in full when, as senior class president, he too was painted green. He was cheer king of the student body, member of the studen
2 CONC t council, athletic reporter for the school paper, school historian, and a member of the track team. In the fall of 1929 he entered Utah State Agricultural College. In the fall of 1930 he was called o
2 CONC n a mission for the L.D.S. Church to the Western States. Being young and having visions of seeing the world, he was disappointed with the call to a mission so close to home. Afterwards, however, he ne
2 CONC ver regretted the decision and was advanced to preside over the Denver District.
2 CONT He and four Elders were privileged to travel over the Mormon Trail. They visited in Independence, Mo., and Carthage, Ill. Here they stayed over night in the jail where the Prophet Joseph and Hyrum Smi
2 CONC th were martyred. That night they surrounded the table at which the Prophet ate, sat in the same chairs, and washed in the same basin. They saw the wooden floors stained by the blood of those men. The
2 CONC y heard the same winds blow. Scenes at the time of the assassination were recounted, and later, in the silence of lamp light, they each wrote a letter to their parents, evidencing their strong testimo
2 CONC nies. In the morning, they left for Nauvoo, Ill., and will always remember their first impression of the city–though beautifully set, a city of the dead–once a thriving urban center, now reeking with 
2 CONC lifelessness. After spending two days there, they drove through Iowa to Omaha, Neb., thence west to Denver.
2 CONT He had many gratifying experiences, broadcasting programs over a radio station at Greeley, Colo., then later in Denver. A number of new Sunday Schools were opened and meetings held, and he was instrum
2 CONC ental in bringing a number of converts into the Church. In the winter of 1933 he again entered Utah State Agricultural College, which schooling proved very satisfying and profitable to him. He was ini
2 CONC tiated a member of Pi Gamma Mu, National Honorary Social Science Fraternity, and graduated in June 1936 with a B.S. degree in the field of Commerce, a major in sociology and a minor in speech.
2 CONT In 1936 he secured employment with the Utah Woolen Mills, with an assignment to work in Oregon and Washington. His career as a traveling salesman came to a dramatic end when on the night of July 5 his
2 CONC  Ford car and all his belongings were stolen. He was left penniless and alone in Hoaquin, Oregon. After three days of searching for the car, which was never found, he hitchhiked home.
2 CONT That fall he accepted a school teaching job at Howell, Box Elder Co., Utah. Later he taught at Grouse Creek, where he was principal. In 1938-9 he began teaching in the elementary school at Tremonton,
2 CONC teaching social studies and supervising the athletic program of the school. In the 3 ½ years of teaching he produced a number of winning teams in football, basketball, baseball, and track. Here he met
2 CONC  his future wife, Reba, and they were married in Logan Temple. In 1943 he decided to quit teaching school and to go into business for himself. He and Reba moved to Malad, Idaho. Here they have been bu
2 CONC sily engaged in Church work. He has been a teacher in Sunday School, the Seventies Quorum, the Scouts, and Superintendent of Sunday School, which position he is now holding. The phase of his life that
2 CONC  he particularly enjoys is teaching and he frequently finds himself a substitute teacher in the Malad schools. He has been called upon frequently as a speaker in the vocational guidance groups at the 
2 CONC school. He enjoys working with students and sometimes wonders if he should have made teaching his life’s work.
2 CONT The advent of Reba’s birth was a joyous occasion in the Roundy family as three sturdy boys had preceded her and a girl was much to be desired. When she was only a year old, her father decided to leave
2 CONC  his position as clerk in the Spanish Fork Co-op Store and move onto a truck garden farm located a short distance from American Fork, in a part of Highland Ward. She remembers the watermelons raised o
2 CONC n that farm, and how friends and neighbors came from far and near to buy them. A large truck was loaded to the brim with the bright green, shining melons, and sent to Salt Lake to market. The boys bro
2 CONC ught her many pennies to put in her quart bottle bank.
2 CONT She had to ride about 12 miles to school and walk one to reach the bus. Some mornings she almost froze before the bus was reached. She enjoyed singing on programs at school and whenever an opportunity
2 CONC  presented itself. Her music teacher, Mr. Nye, encouraged her to take private lessons.
2 CONT Reba had the happy experience of being voted Queen of the Fielding Ward Gold and Green Ball, and marching in the Stake Gold and Green Ball with 12 other Queens, and with Veryl as her partner in both e
2 CONC vents. The evening of their marriage in Logan Temple they had a wedding dance in the Fielding Ward Chapel.
2 CONT In 1952 she had an accident while trying to push the car backward down the driveway toward the house. The car started going too fast and she tried to jump in and stop it. She was thrown underneath, th
2 CONC e car ran over her left leg, and her right one was badly bruised. It was late at night and the family was asleep. She called and called because she could not move. Finally Veryl heard her and rushed o
2 CONC ut in his night clothes. She was carried into the house, and the doctor said no bones were broken, but it took several months for the bruises to heal and for her to get on her feet again.
2 CONT Her Church affiliations have been in the Primary organization. As drama director of the Third Ward M.I.A. she directed a number of plays and skits. She became rather efficient as make-up artist and wa
2 CONC s invited to give demonstrations in a number of wards and throughout the stake. Then she was asked to be Activity Counselor in the Malad Stake Y.L.M.I.A. She enjoyed this work very much and has made m
2 CONC any friends and contacts with people. It has given her a better understanding of the part the Church plays in one’s life.
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 6 July 1919 in the irrigation ditch west of Garland, end. 17 Oct. 1930; bapt. 19 June 1927, end. 9 June 1939, S.to H. 21 June 1939
