1 NAME Donald H. /Fotheringham/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 25 DEC 1905
2 PLAC Panguitch, Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.823
4 LONG W112.436
2 NOTE As written: 25 Dec. 1905
1 NOTE Don attended Garfield High School at Panguitch, and later Provo High School where he was an outstanding athlete as a member of the track and basketball squads. He later transferred to Brigham Young Hi
2 CONC gh School because of an opportunity for work as janitor at the college. During his 1 ½ years at college he paid his own expenses, serving as janitor and as a paper boy with a route of 112 blocks. He w
2 CONC as a good student but had little time for school activities or social accomplishments.
2 CONT Don and Ruth started keeping company when they were students at Garfield High School. He was a member of the basketball squad and she was cheerleader of the school. After their marriage they made thei
2 CONC r home in Panguitch. Later they lived at their Uncle Jim Henrie’s home. Don worked at various jobs, farming, herding sheep, fur trapping. During the depression, when employment was almost at a standst
2 CONC ill, Don joined the C.C.C. He enjoyed his work with the Forest Service very much, was in St. George on a C.C.C. project for about a year, then at Veyo and Cannonville. During these years they purchase
2 CONC d and paid for a home and lot, a car, had three lovely, healthy children, and had established a good credit rating in the locality. Don was next employed as a caterpillar operator for Whiting Construc
2 CONC tion Co. Later he held a similar position for the Bracken Construction Co. at Mercur, Utah. They moved to Mercur and lived in the crude mining camp in a one-room house made of galvanized iron. These w
2 CONC ere happy years, however. Together with other young folks in the camp, they asked for and received permission from the Tooele Stake to organize various auxiliaries of the Church. Don served as Ward Te
2 CONC acher. Ruth served as Primary and Sunday School teacher, and as 2nd counselor in Relief Society.
2 CONT In 1940 Don accepted work on a construction job near Eugene, Ore., but about a month after he started, he was seriously injured and was in the Oregon Hospital for about 1½ years. In 1942, after condit
2 CONC ions had smoothed out, Don set up a small cafe in gingham, Utah, called “Don’s.” He did very well, and one year later moved it to Provo. It was also called “Don’s Cafe.” This was a very successful ven
2 CONC ture, during the eight years they operated it, They bought and paid for a home on Farr Ave., in Provo. Don became a member of the Provo Lions and Ruth a Lady Lions. He served on various committees, he
2 CONC aded the entertainment committee for two years, was director of Timpanogas Dancing Club, Lion Tamer, member of the Rod and Gun Club, and Wild Life Assn.
2 CONT In 1948 they sold the cafe in Provo and purchased the Chief Timp Service Station and Motel in Orem, Utah. They have had good and bad times, but it is with a great deal of satisfaction they note that t
2 CONC oday Dun and Bradstreet list them among the best credit ratings in Orem.
2 CONT For the past four years Ruth has served as a block teacher for Relief Society, with 100 per cent record of visits, She has been theology teacher and special interest leader and Bible teacher in M.I.A.
2 CONT Navy and served during the first part of the Korean War on the Carrier Boxer. He was in charge of air conditioning and refrigeration. The ship was in the theatre of war during most of the 2½ years Ted
2 CONC  served on her; he was a petty officer 1st class when he was honorably discharged. He is now attending the School of Fine Arts, studying Architecture at the University of Utah. On 12 Dec. 1953, Ted ma
2 CONC rried Elaine Blackburn, at Springfield, Idaho, her home. They They had one child:
