1 NAME Grant Delos Alvey /Fotheringham/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 26 OCT 1925
2 PLAC Escalante, Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.77
4 LONG W111.602
2 NOTE As written: 26 Oct. 1925
1 NOTE After finishing high school, Delos was inducted into the Navy and went to Camp Farragut, Idaho. Upon completion of his basic training there, he was transferred to Norfolk, Virginia, given overseas tra
2 CONC ining, and sent from there to the African and European treaters. Just to say that he was sent overseas is a gross understatement. It took thirty days to make the trip, an unforgettable experience. At 
2 CONC times they thought the ship would never make it, and at times they did not care if it did or not. Until they reached the Rock of Gibraltar, they did not know where they were going. They later discover
2 CONC ed they were to land at a small bombed out port of Bazerty in Tunisia.
2 CONT After reaching Bazerty he decided that being a seaman was all right, but that there was a better opportunity for him as a Yeoman. He then started training for the position and after 4 months of hard w
2 CONC ork, he received his third class rating. After some time he was transferred from Yeoman to Motor Machinist, which was more to his liking.
2 CONT During this time the Allies had fought their way up the mainland of Italy and were ready to invade France, so to France their ships of the amphibious force went. After the invasion they were stationed
2 CONC  at Toulone. While there they were privileged to see some of the French country and the way the people lived. As usual, they were moving, to Leghorn, Bari, Toronto, Ancona, then back to the Island of 
2 CONC Sicily where they were stationed at Palarmo.
2 CONT After the war was over in Europe, Delos was sent back to the States for more training for the Pacific conflict, but the war there ceased before he got there.
2 CONT Upon receiving his discharge, he went to work at the Geneva Steel Co. at Orem, Utah. After working as a laborer for a year, he decided to improve his working conditions. He took an apprenticeship cour
2 CONC se in Roll Turning at [p. 375½] the plant, and after four years of hard work and study he was able to accomplish it. He is presently employed at Geneva Steel Co. as a machinist.
2 CONT His wife Paula descended from Utah pioneer stock who had migrated to Old Mexico and later returned to live in Arizona. She has been an inspiration to Delos in increasing his interest in the L.D.S. Chu
2 CONC rch and its teachings. They had 2 children, b. in Provo, Utah:
