1 NAME James Arthur /Henrie/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 MAY 1907
2 PLAC Panguitch, Utah
3 MAP
4 LATI N37.823
4 LONG W112.436
2 NOTE As written: 19 May 1907
1 NOTE Arthur attended grade and high school in Panguitch and had a perfect attendance record until the 8th grade, never having been absent or tardy during those years. Then he and some friends played “hooke
2 CONC y” and he broke his record.
2 CONT In 1925 he moved from Panguitch with his parents and brothers and sisters, to Eugene, Oregon. They arrived at their destination May 30, which was Decoration Day. He completed his high schooling in Eug
2 CONC ene, then went to work as a clerk in a shoe store. Later he went to Portland, Oregon, as an employee of Safeway Stores, for Lester Henrie, a cousin of his father’s. He worked for some time at that pla
2 CONC ce, then married and found employment at various places.
2 CONT As a very small child he loved flowers. Every weed with a blossom was a thing of beauty to him, and he plucked all the early and late ones to carry to his mother for her admiration and thanks. When th
2 CONC e opportunity was presented for him to go into the nursery and landscaping business, he eagerly accepted the offer. The many lovely landscaping and garden jobs he has done in and around Portland, wher
2 CONC e he has made his home, have satisfied his childhood love for nature and beauty. He has made the surroundings of many new homes a thing of beauty, vivid with life and color, the red hat he always wear
2 CONC s being part of the scheme.
2 CONT Besides gardening, he worked in the shipyard at Portland during World War II, when the ships were brought in for repairs. On one occasion while working on or near a huge ventilator, he fell almost int
2 CONC o the shaft, and it was a miracle he ever escaped. His leg was badly crushed from the knee to his foot and for many long months he lay in the hospital, undergoing many operations to splice bones and r
2 CONC emove splintered pieces. After about 2½ years he was well and he now limps but a very little. How a leg could be mended after being crushed as his was is almost beyond belief.
2 CONT During the time of his confinement, he worked in his garden and cleared a city lot of wild blackberry bushes, trees, and all the undergrowth that Oregon’s wet climate can produce. The lot was a verita
2 CONC ble jungle. He built a stool which he strapped to his hips, fashioned a long sock, made from an old inner tube, pulled it up over his hip length cast, and with the usual rain equipment of rubber coat,
2 CONC  hat, gloves, and foot wear, he managed to clear that entire lot, except for a few boulders and large stumps that required a caterpillar to handle. He said when he was thus busy he partly forgot the p
2 CONC ain and suffering.
2 CONT He married Maxine Thompson, and they made themselves a modest home in the
2 CONT northeast suburbs of Portland. They both enjoy gardening, vegetables and flowers. Maxine cans the produce from the garden and has a full larder all the time. Arthur loves to hunt and fish, and the lar
2 CONC ge salmon he catches almost every year and the venison go a long way toward a plentiful living.
2 CONT For many years Maxine was a clothing remodeler for J. C. Penney Co. and was expert at her work. She is an ideal homemaker and mother to their two little children. They were married for twelve years be
2 CONC fore a child came to bless their home, and then a boy to keep the girl company constitute the family. Dianne is the duplicate of her mother in complexion and features, dark hair and eyes. The boy, Jam
2 CONC es Arthur III, called Jamie and J.A., is like his father, light complexioned, with blue eyes. Arthur’s parents long to have the family closer by where they could watch the growth and development of th
2 CONC e children:
1 NOTE LDS ordinances as recorded in the book: bapt. 31 July 1915
